From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 01:03:35 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] bpf: run callbacks of interpreted programs

Helpers such as bpf_loop(), bpf_for_each_map_elem(), bpf_find_vma(),
bpf_user_ringbuf_drain() and the BPF timers call a program's callback
as a native bpf_callback_t. A JIT hands them the compiled subprogram,
but an interpreted subprogram has no code of its own, so
fixup_call_args() rejects every interpreted program that loads a
callback. A kernel that must not generate code at run time has no JIT
to fall back on, and loses all of these helpers.

Build a fixed pool of 1024 native entry points into the interpreter
instead. Each entry point looks up its slot and enters the interpreter
at the bound callback subprogram with that subprogram's stack frame,
exactly as BPF_CALL_ARGS enters it for a bpf-to-bpf call. The verifier
binds one slot to each callback subprogram of an interpreted program,
and bpf_prog_free_deferred() releases them once the program can no
longer run, which also covers a load that fails after binding. A load
that finds every slot bound fails with -ENOSPC. The helpers are
unchanged, so every present and future callback site works.

The verifier's call depth, stack, recursion and bpf_loop inlining rules
are enforced before fixup_call_args() and are unchanged. Tail calls
reachable from a subprogram, including a callback, and kfunc calls stay
rejected for interpreted programs. Offloaded programs keep rejecting
callbacks.

An instruction dump shows a bound callback load as jit_subprogs() leaves
it, as the subprogram's relative offset and number, never an entry
point address. When jit_subprogs() falls back to the interpreter it now
restores callback loads as well as calls.
